# Pinning for the qemu-pmb887x wasm build.
#
# The qemu source is the root-level qemu/ submodule (Azq2/qemu-pmb887x,
# local branch wasm-browser-port = origin/wasm-patches): the whole
# wasm/TCI/perf series committed on top of qemu-pmb887x master
# (8b9d485bc2), plus the AFE fix cherry-picked from alula's dsp-stuff
# branch and the per-board RTC-seed commit. (The former patches/*.patch
# files were removed on 2026-09-12; the commits are the series.)
# The pmb887x-emu meta-repo is checked out alongside (its own qemu
# submodule pin is the same revision) but is not built from.
#
# Why not plain master: master alone aborts every Siemens fullflash
# during L1 GSM frame handling (">>EXIT<< FILE: l1bbcsg / CepName:
# H_GSMFR" at ~25-30 s — the L1<->DSP handshake starves); the "hacky AFE
# (LLE+HLE)" commit from dsp-stuff fixes s75/el71/c81.
#   qemu b7822c674b (2026-09-15 two merges.  alula/dsp-stuff came in
#                    resolved to ours and changed no file: its two content
#                    commits are already here verbatim (the AFE cherry-pick
#                    has the same patch-id as alula's, and debfa3d6f5 is his
#                    RTC-seed commit), and what was left on his side is a
#                    dsp_hexdump() that indexes words where the offset
#                    counts bytes, the pre-fix dsp_realize(), and two #if 0
#                    debug blocks.  The merge is recorded so the branch
#                    stops looking behind.
#                    The one that matters is qemu-pmb887x master, plus a
#                    whitespace fix on top.  Master
#                    brought one commit that counts here, "pmb887x: Fix
#                    dsp crash on Siemens SGOLD phones" (PR #4): it lands
#                    inside dsp.c's #ifdef STUB_DSP block, which upstream
#                    leaves disabled and this branch enables, so it is
#                    inert there and live here.  dsp_realize() now seeds
#                    DSP shared-RAM word 0 from rom_version - the value
#                    board/dsp.c actually sets from the board's
#                    dsp.ram0_value - instead of the ram0_value *property*,
#                    which nothing ever sets and whose default is 0x0801.
#                    The boards that ask for 0x0602, siemens-cx75 and
#                    include/siemens-sgl-r65, get their own value for the
#                    first time; every other board already wanted 0x0801,
#                    which is why this never showed up on s75/el71/c81)
#   qemu cd0ecd359d (2026-09-15 "wasm-diag: gate the counters that ended
#                    up on hot paths (0076)" - the tip of the series.
#                    Round fourteen, 0074-0076, is about what it costs to
#                    stay unwindable.  -sASYNCIFY_ONLY names every
#                    function Asyncify must instrument for a coroutine
#                    switch, and instrumenting one is not free; the list
#                    still held eighteen entries reachable only from the
#                    vCPU thread, where rr_cpu_thread_fn() forbids
#                    coroutines outright and qemu_coroutine_switch()
#                    aborts, because the JIT'd TB frame below a helper
#                    cannot be instrumented at all.  They were the stack
#                    of a vCPU flash write, the case flash-blk.c had
#                    already moved to a main-loop bottom half - the fix
#                    deleted the stack, nothing deleted the entries.
#                    0074 removes them (verified with QEMU_COSTACK=1 and
#                    the new tools/asyncify-audit.mjs: 67 distinct frames
#                    on a 120 s S75 boot, all still covered, none of the
#                    eighteen among them).  0075 folds the MMIO fast path
#                    into its callers with a compile-time size, which
#                    also collapses a double bswap that cancelled on
#                    every little-endian device read.  0076 gates the
#                    ~14M diagnostic read-modify-writes a second that had
#                    accumulated on the hot paths (rebuild with
#                    WASM_DIAG_HOT_COUNTERS to get the rates back, and
#                    never wall-clock A/B such a build).  S75 idle vs
#                    round thirteen: +8.5 % MIPS, +8.4 % v/wall (4/4
#                    pairs); EL71 +3.4 % / +3.1 % (3/3) - less, because
#                    EL71 is compute-bound and spends little time in the
#                    functions this round un-instrumented.  See
#                    doc/performance-handoff.md)
#   qemu 4368d0317b (2026-09-15 "system/cpus: do not give the BQL back
#                    after every device access (0073)" - round thirteen,
#                    0070-0073, is mostly one
#                    finding: wasm has no relaxed atomic and no acquire
#                    fence, so every qatomic_set is a seq_cst
#                    iN.atomic.store and every smp_rmb() an atomic.fence,
#                    and QEMU pays for ordering it does not need here.
#                    0070 read the virtual clock without the seqlock's
#                    fence pair or the atomic publish (icount_get
#                    4.3 % -> 0.6 % of the vCPU; a ceiling probe said
#                    +5.0 % was available and the patch took +5.2 %),
#                    0072 did the same for cpu_handle_interrupt's
#                    per-pass qatomic_set_mb plus three other fixed
#                    costs, and 0073 stopped handing the BQL back after
#                    every one of the S75's three million device
#                    accesses a second (bounded by the rr loop's own
#                    unlock before every tcg_cpu_exec, so a missed
#                    release is one icount slice of delay, not a
#                    deadlock).  0071 is the other kind: a short hflags
#                    rebuild for a CPU with no M/AArch64/EL2/EL3/PMSA/v6,
#                    verified by a build that takes the short path,
#                    computes the generic answer anyway and counts
#                    disagreements - ~59M rebuilds, zero.  S75 idle vs
#                    round twelve: +14.6 % MIPS, +14.1 % v/wall (4/4
#                    pairs); EL71 +10.0 % / +9.9 % (3/3); KE800 boot
#                    -10 %.  See
#                    doc/performance-handoff.md)
#   bsp  21fdacb    (2026-09-15 master merged into rtc-calendar-format.
#                    Master's 55752c5 comments out the [peripheral.RF]
#                    hd155153np entries on the x75 and ELKA includes — the
#                    device the emulator has no table entry for, which used
#                    to abort board init with 'hardware error: Unknown
#                    device: hd155153np' and which this project carried a
#                    bsp-patches/ workaround for until now — and maps
#                    CAMERA and PLAY_PAUSE into the S75 keypad matrix, so
#                    the two side keys the page already draws finally reach
#                    the firmware.  The branch keeps e6e73d1, "LG boards
#                    read the RTC counter as packed calendar fields": the
#                    [rtc] format = "calendar" the LG firmware needs, the
#                    companion of qemu's RTC cnt-format commit, which
#                    master does not have)
export QEMU_PMB887X_REPO="https://github.com/Azq2/qemu-pmb887x"
export QEMU_PMB887X_REV="b7822c674bcb70586c2885d7059f5966ef4855ed"
export QEMU_PMB887X_BRANCH="wasm-browser-port"

export PMB887X_BSP_REPO="https://github.com/Azq2/pmb887x-dev"
export PMB887X_BSP_REV="21fdacbd44822d1c0ed4f96e2266e4b9f3c73f41"

# Toolchain versions (mirror qemu's tests/docker/dockerfiles/emsdk-wasm64-cross.docker)
export EMSDK_VERSION="4.0.10"
export ZLIB_VERSION="1.3.2"
export PIXMAN_VERSION="0.44.2"
export LIBFFI_VERSION="v3.5.2"
export GLIB_VERSION="2.84.0"
